# L1 / B003 — instruction map and level rules.

C R46 OL RUN-001 | DEFAULT | Write clear, correct code first; add specialized optimizations only for a concrete reason, preferring measurement or profiling evidence for non-trivial ones. | backend code | none stated
C R47 OL RUN-008 | DEFAULT | Introduce compiled queries, allocation-reducing return types (ValueTask), pooling, custom serialization, aggressive caching or raw SQL for performance only for a measured or clearly demonstrated need. | backend code | none stated
C R48 OL RUN-006 | DEFAULT | Keep database work efficient: filter and project in the database when practical, avoid unnecessary round trips, over-fetching and N+1 patterns, and paginate potentially large result sets. | backend data access | none stated
C R49 OL RUN-002 | MUST | Use async APIs for asynchronous I/O and never block them with synchronous waits (.Result, .Wait(), .GetAwaiter().GetResult()) or similar sync-over-async patterns. | backend code | none stated
C R50 OL RUN-003 | MUST | Propagate cancellation tokens through asynchronous operations when cancellation is meaningful (at the entry point HttpContext.RequestAborted); do not add cancellation parameters to purely synchronous code without a use case. | backend code | none stated
C R51 OL RUN-004 | DEFAULT | Use an injectable time abstraction (TimeProvider; tests FakeTimeProvider) for logic that depends on the current clock and needs deterministic tests; do not mechanically replace stored or explicit timestamps. | backend code | none stated
C R52 OL RUN-005 | MUST NOT | Never create and dispose a new HTTP client instance per request; use a factory-managed client (IHttpClientFactory, typed or named clients) or another appropriate long-lived strategy. | outbound HTTP | none stated
C R53 OL RUN-015 | MUST | External I/O calls have a bounded execution time appropriate to the operation; avoid overlapping timeout mechanisms whose combined behavior is unclear. | outbound dependencies | none stated
C R54 OL RUN-016 | MUST | Retry only when repeating the operation is safe; never automatically retry state-changing operations without idempotency or another concrete duplicate-prevention mechanism. | outbound dependencies | none stated
C R55 OL RUN-017 | DEFAULT | Respect Retry-After or equivalent rate-limit guidance where appropriate; never retry indefinitely or aggressively. | outbound dependencies | none stated
C R56 OL RUN-018 | MUST | Use fallback only when the fallback result is semantically acceptable; never hide correctness failures behind convenient default values. | outbound dependencies | none stated
C R57 OL RUN-019 | MUST NOT | Caller cancellation is not a transient dependency failure: never retry it or convert it into repeated outbound attempts. | outbound dependencies | none stated
C R58 OL RUN-007 | DEFAULT | Caching is not a default architectural requirement; introduce it only for a clear measured or reasoned need and choose the implementation by deployment, consistency and staleness requirements. | backend caching | none stated
C R59 OL RUN-020 | MUST | Cache keys uniquely represent the cached value and include the relevant user, tenant, locale and query dimensions; never use global keys for user- or tenant-specific data. | backend caching | none stated
C R60 OL RUN-023 | MUST | Every cache entry has a lifetime or another explicit invalidation strategy. | backend caching | none stated
C R61 OL RUN-021 | MUST | Invalidate or update cache entries only consistently with successful underlying mutations; the cache is never a second source of truth. | backend caching | none stated
C R62 OL RUN-022 | DEFAULT | Use strongly typed options for structured service configuration (IOptions<T> bound with AddOptions<T>().Bind), validated at startup (ValidateOnStart) when missing or invalid values prevent correct or secure operation; avoid scattered string-based configuration lookups. | backend configuration | none stated
C R63 OL ARC-021 | DEFAULT | Use constructor injection for required dependencies; avoid service-locator and property-injection patterns. | backend DI | none stated
C R64 OL ARC-022 | MUST | Respect service lifetimes: never capture a shorter-lived dependency inside a longer-lived service. | backend DI | none stated
C R65 OL RUN-013 | DEFAULT | Log through the injected logging abstraction (ILogger<T>) with structured message templates and stable property names; avoid string interpolation that turns structured values into opaque text. | backend logging | none stated
C R66 OL RUN-009 | DEFAULT | Follow the repository's conventions and .editorconfig; use modern C# when it improves clarity, but do not mechanically modernize working code. | C# code | none stated
C R67 OL RUN-024 | DEFAULT | For new code prefer file-scoped namespaces when consistent with the project, keep one primary top-level type per matching file, and use the Async suffix for task-returning (awaitable) methods unless an existing or framework contract makes that inappropriate. | new C# code | Existing or framework contracts.
C R68 OL RUN-011 | MUST NOT | Do not perform style-only rewrites outside the requested change unless explicitly asked. | C# code | Explicit request.
C R69 OL DEP-001 | MUST | Add a dependency only for a concrete current requirement; never install packages for hypothetical future use. | all dependencies | none stated
C R70 OL FE-002 | MUST | Keep pages and components focused on presentation and UI orchestration; do not move trivial presentation logic into services merely to shrink components. | frontend | none stated
C R71 OL FE-003 | MUST | Keep backend communication in explicit feature data-access services; do not scatter HTTP client calls through presentation components and never import backend domain or persistence types into the frontend. | frontend | none stated
C R72 OL FE-007 | MUST | Frontend structure stays feature-oriented: avoid application-wide technical buckets and empty architecture folders, keep feature-specific code inside its feature, keep core/shared small. | frontend | none stated
C R73 OL FE-005 | DEFAULT | Use route-level lazy loading for meaningful features; do not lazy-load every tiny component. | frontend routing | none stated
C R74 OL FE-006 | DEFAULT | Prefer simple local, component or service state before introducing a state-management library. | frontend state | none stated
C R75 OL FE-009 | MUST | Follow the documented API error contract: centralize reusable problem-details parsing when practical and never infer business behavior from human-readable exception messages. | frontend HTTP error handling | none stated
C R76 OL FE-010 | MUST | Use reactive forms for non-trivial forms when explicit state, validation and testability help; frontend validation improves UX but never replaces backend validation or data-integrity and security enforcement. | frontend forms | none stated
C R77 OL API-002 | DEFAULT | Return problem-details responses (RFC 9457; ProblemDetails via AddProblemDetails) for non-validation HTTP failures and validation problem-details (ValidationProblemDetails, automatic [ApiController] response) for request validation; keep machine-readable error contracts stable and safe. | HTTP API | none stated
C R78 OL API-003 | MUST | Choose 200/201/202/204 by actual HTTP semantics: never 200 for every outcome, 201 merely because a row was inserted internally, or 202 for work that already completed. | HTTP API | none stated
C R79 OL API-004 | DEFAULT | A successful collection query with zero matches returns 200 with an empty collection, not 404. | HTTP API | none stated
C R80 OL API-005 | MUST | Use 401 when authentication is missing or failed and 403 when an authenticated principal is not permitted; returning 404 to hide protected resources requires a deliberate, consistent security policy. | HTTP API | none stated
C R81 OL API-006 | MUST | Use 409 for a valid request that conflicts with current state and 412 when an explicit HTTP precondition such as If-Match fails. | HTTP API | none stated
C R82 OL API-007 | DEFAULT | Unexpected failures propagate to one centralized exception-handling boundary (IExceptionHandler with AddExceptionHandler/UseExceptionHandler); avoid repetitive catch/log/rethrow blocks. | backend failures | none stated
C R83 OL API-008 | MUST NOT | Never expose stack traces, SQL, connection strings, internal paths, secret values or sensitive exception details in public API errors. | HTTP API errors | none stated
C R84 OL API-011 | MUST NOT | Do not establish business exceptions as the default architecture, and do not throw and catch exceptions to jump between normal expected branches. | backend code | none stated
C R85 OL API-012 | MUST | Log an unexpected exception once, at the boundary that handles it, not again in repository, use-case, entry-point and global handler. | backend failures | none stated
C R86 OL API-021 | MUST NOT | Normal client-request cancellation (OperationCanceledException with RequestAborted signaled) is not a server failure: never map it to a generic 500 or log it as an unhandled application error. | HTTP API | none stated
C R87 OL API-013 | MUST NOT | Do not introduce API versioning merely because an API exists; add it when published compatibility or coexisting client generations create a concrete need. | HTTP API versioning | none stated
C R88 OL API-014 | MUST | Once external clients rely on an API version, avoid silent breaking changes: a breaking contract change requires a new version or another explicitly approved compatibility strategy. | published HTTP APIs | Explicitly approved compatibility strategy.
C R89 OL API-015 | DEFAULT | When explicit versioning is required, use one strategy consistently: URL-segment versioning unless project requirements justify another. | HTTP API versioning | Project requirements.
C R90 OL API-018 | MUST | Health checks stay cheap and deterministic: never full business workflows, expensive queries or mutating database work. | health endpoints | none stated
C R91 OL API-019 | MUST | Liveness avoids external dependencies whose outage could cause restart loops; readiness may depend on capabilities required to serve traffic and reflects startup and dependency availability. | health endpoints | none stated
C R92 OL API-020 | MUST NOT | Publicly reachable health responses must not expose secrets, credentials, connection details, stack traces or unnecessary internal diagnostics. | health endpoints | none stated
C R93 OL SEC-001 | MUST NOT | Never hardcode or commit real secrets, credentials, private keys, access/refresh tokens or production connection strings, including in example or Development configuration files. | all files | none stated
C R94 OL SEC-002 | DEFAULT | Use a local secret mechanism in development (dotnet user-secrets) and environment-specific injection or a managed secret store in deployed environments; do not assume a cloud provider the project has not chosen. | secrets | none stated
C R95 OL SEC-003 | MUST | Treat data crossing the trusted boundary (HTTP requests, uploads, external-service responses, message payloads, user-supplied identifiers and filters) as untrusted and validate it at the owning layer where assumptions matter. | input handling | none stated
C R96 OL SEC-004 | MUST | Use parameterized database access; never build SQL by concatenating untrusted input. | data access | none stated
C R97 OL SEC-015 | MUST NOT | Do not trust client-provided file metadata such as MIME type as authoritative when upload handling or security decisions depend on actual content. | uploads | none stated
C R98 OL SEC-005 | MUST | Enforce authorization on the server; frontend route guards, hidden or disabled buttons and client checks are UX, not a security boundary. | authorization | none stated
C R99 OL SEC-006 | MUST | Anonymous access is deliberate and visible; avoid redundant authorization attributes when a secure global or fallback policy already expresses the intent. | authorization | none stated
C R100 OL SEC-016 | MUST NOT | Do not implement custom token or password security, password hashing or reset-token cryptography when the platform's established authentication and identity facilities (ASP.NET Core authentication handlers, ASP.NET Core Identity) cover the requirement. | authentication | none stated
C R101 OL SEC-017 | MUST | For Bearer/OIDC authentication validate what the actual issuer requires (issuer, audience where applicable, signature/signing authority, lifetime); never disable validation to make a token work. | authentication | none stated
C R102 OL SEC-018 | MUST NOT | Do not expose authentication-failure detail that unnecessarily reveals secrets or account state; login behavior avoids account-enumeration leaks where relevant. | authentication | none stated
C R103 OL SEC-011 | MUST | When browser cookies carry authentication credentials automatically, include CSRF protection in the design; do not assume the frontend framework removes the risk. | cookie authentication | none stated
C R104 OL SEC-012 | MUST NOT | Do not claim any browser token-storage mechanism is universally secure; choose storage by the application's threat model; tokens are never logged. | browser token storage | none stated
C R105 OL SEC-007 | MUST | Production traffic carrying credentials or sensitive data uses TLS; respect the actual hosting topology (TLS may terminate at a proxy) and never disable certificate validation to make an integration work. | transport | none stated
C R106 OL SEC-009 | DEFAULT | Configure CORS as the narrowest policy satisfying actual browser-client requirements; never combine credentialed cross-origin requests with unrestricted origins. | CORS | none stated
C R107 OL SEC-008 | MUST NOT | Do not implement custom cryptography when a well-reviewed platform mechanism fits; do not treat the platform's application payload-protection API (ASP.NET Core Data Protection, short-lived payloads) as universal database encryption. | cryptography | none stated
C R108 OL SEC-010 | MUST NOT | Never log passwords, access/refresh tokens, private keys, authentication secrets or full payment credentials; avoid PII without a concrete permitted operational need; log level is not a privacy control. | logging | none stated
C R109 OL RUN-014 | MUST NOT | Do not serialize arbitrary request bodies, domain objects or sensitive payloads into logs by default; log the minimum operationally useful non-sensitive context. | logging | none stated
C R110 OL SEC-013 | DEFAULT | Security-sensitive authorization, permission and required-control checks fail safely; never silently convert a lookup or configuration failure into success. | security checks | none stated
C R111 OL SEC-014 | MUST | When a required security control cannot be configured correctly, prefer a visible startup/configuration failure over an insecure fallback. | security configuration | none stated
C R112 OL TST-002 | DEFAULT | Choose the narrowest test level that meaningfully verifies the behavior (unit tests for isolated logic, integration tests when framework wiring, HTTP, persistence, serialization, authentication or component interaction is the subject); no fixed unit/integration ratio is required. | backend tests | none stated
C R113 OL TST-003 | MUST | Test observable behavior, not implementation details: tests survive internal refactoring; do not assert private calls or incidental call sequences or reproduce the implementation's algorithm; interaction checks only when the interaction is the specified behavior. | backend tests | Interaction is the specified behavior.
C R114 OL TST-004 | MUST | Tests are deterministic, independent of execution order and isolated from Production and Development data, uncontrolled external systems, shared mutable state and (where an injectable time abstraction allows) wall-clock timing. | backend tests | none stated
C R115 OL TST-005 | DEFAULT | For small finite input/state spaces list explicit declarative cases (an NUnit [TestCase] row; TestCaseSource counts as generated case machinery) instead of loops, branching, LINQ or generated case machinery inside tests, and state expected results instead of computing them. | backend tests | none stated
C R116 OL TST-006 | DEFAULT | Use in-process host integration tests (WebApplicationFactory<Program>, created in [OneTimeSetUp] and disposed in [OneTimeTearDown]) when routing, binding, middleware, authentication, DI, repositories, the ORM or database persistence is what the test must prove. | backend integration tests | none stated
C R117 OL TST-008 | MUST | Tests start from a known state and own their setup; they never depend on development seed data or on the order of other tests. | backend tests | none stated
C R118 OL TST-011 | MUST NOT | Do not generate low-value tests merely to raise a coverage percentage. | backend tests | none stated
C R119 OL TST-012 | DEFAULT | Use descriptive test names stating scenario/condition and expected behavior (e.g. MethodOrScenario_Condition_ExpectedBehavior); follow an established project convention when one exists. | backend tests | Established project convention.
C R120 OL ARC-001,ARC-006,ARC-012,ARC-016 | DECIDED | The backend follows Clean Architecture with the role-to-project mapping: domain model = backend/<Name>.Domain; use-case layer with Application Services, persistence-access contracts and the commit boundary IUnitOfWork = <Name>.Application; implementation layer (persistence and external adapters) = <Name>.Infrastructure; entry point and composition root (Program.cs) = <Name>.Api; test projects <Name>.Application.Tests and <Name>.IntegrationTests (optional Domain.Tests, Infrastructure.Tests). | backend architecture (always loaded) | none stated
C R121 OL ARC-009 | REQUIRES_APPROVAL | Do not introduce CQRS command/query handlers, mediator libraries or mediator-based request dispatch, Vertical Slice Architecture or similar mediator-based organization unless explicitly approved; a service growing too large is a signal to reconsider its responsibilities, not a reason to adopt another pattern. | backend architecture | Explicit approval.
C R122 OL ARC-002 | MUST | Keep Domain free of persistence-implementation, web-framework/transport-implementation, HTTP and external-service implementation concerns. | Domain project | none stated
C R123 OL ARC-003 | DEFAULT | Simple domain entities by default; straightforward CRUD and workflow rules (validation, immutable fields, status transitions, allowed or forbidden edits) belong in Application Services and do not by themselves justify a Rich Domain Model. | Domain and Application | none stated
C R124 OL ARC-004 | REQUIRES_APPROVAL | Move behavior into Domain only when non-trivial domain invariants or behavior materially benefit from domain-level encapsulation independently of one use case; otherwise a Rich Domain Model or DDD tactical pattern requires approval unless the approved specification already requires it. | Domain | Approved specification requires it.
C R125 OL ARC-005 | MUST NOT | Do not add factory methods, private setters, transition methods, Aggregate Roots, Domain Events, Specifications, Domain Services, systematic Value Objects or similar constructs merely to make a simple model look domain-driven. | Domain | none stated
C R126 OL ARC-006 | DEFAULT | Application Services orchestrate use cases; they may coordinate Domain objects, repositories, external-service abstractions and the Unit of Work. | Application | none stated
C R127 OL ARC-029 | MUST NOT | Do not duplicate equivalent DTOs across Api and Application merely to create layer-shaped types; add transport-specific request/response contracts in Api when separation from Application models gives a concrete boundary benefit. | Api and Application contracts | none stated
C R128 OL ARC-010 | MUST | Entry-point adapters in Api (controllers) are the default HTTP entry point and stay thin: they bind and validate HTTP input, invoke Application Services and translate outcomes into HTTP responses. | Api | none stated
C R129 OL ARC-011 | MUST NOT | Entry-point adapters must not contain business logic, access the persistence context or concrete repositories, or construct infrastructure services manually. | Api | none stated
C R130 OL ARC-013 | DEFAULT | Prefer specific repository interfaces representing cohesive Application persistence needs over generic CRUD contracts mirroring tables; interfaces live in Application, implementations in Infrastructure. | repositories | none stated
C R131 OL ARC-015 | MUST NOT | Application must not depend on IGenericRepository<T> or another generic CRUD contract; a generic repository base class may exist only inside Infrastructure, only to remove real duplication, and never as a replacement for the ORM's query capabilities. | repositories | none stated
C R132 OL ARC-016 | DEFAULT | IUnitOfWork (defined in Application) is the normal commit boundary of a use case; repositories stage changes and Application Services decide when to commit; the implementation delegates to AppDbContext.SaveChangesAsync and staged changes are the shared context's tracked changes. | use-case commits | none stated
C R133 OL ARC-017 | MUST | Repositories taking part in one use case share the same scoped Unit of Work and persistence context (the scoped AppDbContext); inject repositories explicitly; IUnitOfWork must not become a repository container or service locator. | repositories and Unit of Work | none stated
C R134 OL ARC-018 | DEFAULT | Do not introduce explicit transaction APIs until a concrete use case needs control beyond a single commit through IUnitOfWork. | transactions | none stated
C R135 OL API-001 | MUST | HTTP status codes and transport response semantics belong to the entry point; the use-case layer and domain model must not return transport result types (IActionResult, ActionResult<T>, IResult) or HTTP status codes. | backend layers | none stated
C R136 OL API-009 | DEFAULT | Represent common expected outcomes explicitly when useful (null, bool, a typed outcome) but do not force a generic result-wrapper pattern and do not name use-case outcome categories after HTTP statuses; the entry point maps outcomes to HTTP. | use-case outcomes | none stated
C R137 OL ARC-024 | MUST | Keep cache technology out of the domain model and do not leak infrastructure cache APIs into the use-case layer without a deliberate boundary abstraction. | caching boundaries | none stated
C R138 OL ARC-025 | MUST | The implementation layer translates remote HTTP responses (HttpResponseMessage, headers, status) into use-case-layer abstractions; remote transport response objects and other remote transport concerns must not leak into the use-case layer. | outbound HTTP boundaries | none stated
C R139 OL ARC-026 | MUST NOT | Do not create separate domain or persistence models merely because the public API has several versions; isolate version-specific transport contracts at the entry point when practical. | API versioning | none stated
C R140 OL API-010 | MUST | Use FluentValidation for explicit request and use-case validation where appropriate and keep each rule at its owning boundary (transport concerns at the entry point, use-case rules in the use-case layer, domain invariants in the domain model); do not move domain invariants into validators or duplicate one rule mechanically across layers. | validation | none stated
C R141 OL API-016 | MUST | Swagger/OpenAPI metadata describes the API that actually runs (routes, important response statuses including pipeline-produced ones, schemas, authentication behavior); never document speculative responses. | API documentation | none stated
C R142 OL API-017 | DEFAULT | Enable Swagger UI in Development; exposure in other environments follows the Swagger decision (only when deployment requirements explicitly justify it). | API documentation | none stated
C R143 OL DEP-021 | DEFAULT | When Serilog is configured use the DI-integrated AddSerilog approach with provider configuration in environment-aware configuration; application code keeps depending on ILogger<T>. | logging provider | none stated
C R144 OL DEP-019 | DEFAULT | Production containers use an appropriate runtime image rather than the SDK image for convenience and do not switch to root merely to bypass filesystem permission problems. | containers | none stated
C R145 OL ARC-010,DEP-008 | MUST | The entry-point mechanism is ASP.NET Core Controllers (controller classes in a Controllers/ folder of the entry-point project); do not create Minimal API endpoint groups unless explicitly requested. | Api | Explicit request.
C R146 OL FE-001 | DEFAULT | Use modern standalone Angular APIs for new code and organize application code primarily by feature. | frontend | none stated
C R147 OL FE-004 | DEFAULT | Use Signals for naturally synchronous, local UI state and RxJS when asynchronous streams, HTTP composition, cancellation or event pipelines make it clearer; avoid mechanical conversions. | frontend state | none stated
C R148 OL FE-008 | DEFAULT | Prefer functional interceptors for new code; keep interceptor responsibilities cross-cutting and never put feature business logic in an interceptor. | frontend HTTP | none stated
C R149 OL FE-011 | DEFAULT | Avoid manual nested RxJS subscriptions; prefer composition operators when stream composition expresses the behavior clearly. | frontend | none stated
C R150 OL FE-012 | MUST | When manually subscribing from framework-managed code, tie the subscription lifetime to the component or service lifecycle using the project's current Angular facilities. | frontend | none stated
C R151 OL ARC-019,ARC-015 | MUST | EF Core is an implementation-layer detail: use its full capabilities inside the persistence implementation but never leak EF Core concepts into the use-case layer; do not cripple repository implementations to preserve a generic abstraction. | persistence implementation | none stated
C R152 OL ARC-014 | MUST NOT | Persistence-access contracts must not expose IQueryable<T>, DbSet<T>, DbContext, EF Core expressions or provider-specific types; LINQ-to-EF query construction stays in the persistence implementation and contracts return materialized entities, collections, projections or explicit result models. | repository contracts | none stated
C R153 OL PST-001 | DEFAULT | For an empty Development database maintain a small, deterministic, representative seed dataset covering the main meaningful states and relationships (new, active, completed, archived; with and without optional values; boundary states); no exhaustive combinations or random bulk data. | Development seeding | none stated
C R154 OL PST-002 | MUST | Development seeding is a persistence-implementation/bootstrap concern: it may use AppDbContext directly and need not go through persistence-access contracts or the commit boundary. | Development seeding | none stated
C R155 OL PST-003 | MUST | Keep substantial seed construction in a dedicated internal DbSeeder exposed through a narrow initialization extension, not in Program.cs, AppDbContext, entity configurations or migrations. | Development seeding | none stated
C R156 OL PST-004 | MUST NOT | Automatic seeding runs only in the Development environment; never seed Production automatically; tests never use the seeder. | Development seeding | none stated
C R157 OL PST-005 | MUST NOT | The seeder must not call Database.Migrate() or EnsureCreated() or otherwise create or upgrade schema; migrations are applied separately before seeding. | Development seeding | none stated
C R158 OL DEP-002 | MUST NOT | When an approved technology exists in the decisions file, use it rather than silently selecting an alternative; replacing it is Reserved (record with architecture-decision). | technology choices | Approved replacement.
C R159 OL DEP-008 | DECIDED | Backend platform: .NET 10 LTS (latest stable servicing release), C# 14, ASP.NET Core, ASP.NET Core Controllers; moving to a preview/RC .NET or C# release requires approval. | backend platform | Approval for preview/RC.
C R160 OL DEP-010 | DECIDED | FluentValidation is the standard explicit request/use-case validation library; application logging uses ILogger<T> with Serilog as the provider. | validation and logging libraries | none stated
C R161 OL DEP-012,API-017 | DECIDED | Swashbuckle Swagger UI is the interactive API documentation and manual-testing surface for Development (/swagger); expose it outside Development only when deployment requirements explicitly justify it. | API documentation | Deployment requirements.
C R162 OL DEP-011 | DEFAULT | Microsoft resilience packages are approved only when a real integration needs resilience. | resilience packages | none stated
C R163 OL DEP-022 | DEFAULT | Use the .slnx solution format; legacy .sln only when an external tool or project requirement needs it. | solution files | External tool or project requirement.
C R164 OL DEP-023 | DEFAULT | Use repository-level Directory.Build.props for genuinely shared .NET build settings and Directory.Packages.props for Central Package Management; project-specific settings stay in their own projects. | .NET build configuration | none stated
C R165 OL DEP-018 | MUST NOT | Do not introduce Docker merely because the application can be containerized; add container artifacts only when the project or deployment needs them. | containers | none stated
C R166 OL DEP-009 | DECIDED | Persistence is EF Core with Microsoft SQL Server (Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.SqlServer); provider-specific concerns stay out of the use-case layer and domain model. | persistence stack | none stated
C R167 OL DEP-013 | DECIDED | Backend testing: NUnit; backend test projects live under backend/ beside production projects; WebApplicationFactory<Program> is the integration-test baseline; Testcontainers is conditional. | backend testing stack | none stated
C R168 OL DEP-026,SEC-001 | DEFAULT | appsettings.Development.json provides a usable local Development connection string whenever it contains no secret credentials (e.g. LocalDB with Trusted_Connection); when local SQL Server requires credentials keep the non-secret structure in configuration and supply the credential-bearing value through user-secrets or another approved local secret mechanism. | Development configuration | none stated
C X12 EX - | MUST (checker-enforced) | Level model of the instruction system: every artifact is placed by stability (0-core, 1-architecture, 2-technology, 3-project) and starts with a one-line header (Level, optional Capability/Facet, Owns, Scope, Loading); conformance dependencies point only to the same or a more stable level; Core and Architecture name no concrete implementation technology in any form, pointers included, except the fixed platform vocabulary (.NET, C#, TypeScript, Git, HTTP; ASP.NET Core and NuGet are not exempt) and refer to Level 2 only by role, capability, facet or a technology-neutral skill name; every artifact is listed in the instruction map, and check-instructions is run after any change. | authoring or changing instruction artifacts (template maintenance) | none stated | Instruction-system organization rule of the L1 template; the oracle defines no level hierarchy, header format or technology-neutrality requirement for instruction files. | Adding a sentence naming EF Core or a pointer '(technique: ef-core)' to a Core rule fails the checker and must be moved to a Level 2 facet.
C X13 EX ARC-020,DEP-009 | MUST | Static capability composition: exactly four slots (architecture required frame; persistence and backend-testing required leaves; frontend optional leaf); each required slot has exactly one selected implementation and the optional slot zero or one; only selected modules ship; module-owned artifacts declare Capability: and appear in their MODULE.md; a <slot>.<facet> reference resolves through the selection record and a cleared optional slot (none) means its facets do not apply. | template capability structure and selection record | none stated | No oracle policy defines capability slots, their cardinality or a selection record; ARC-020/DEP-009 only fix the approved stack and require approval to change it. | A maintainer may not add a fifth slot (e.g. logging), keep two persistence implementations, or ship an unselected module.
C X14 EX - | MUST (checker-enforced) | Hooks are registered in exec form (command node with the script path as argument data, no shell); the critical PreToolUse gate sets onFailure block so a missing script, crash, timeout or malformed input blocks the command; PostToolUse and Stop hooks stay non-blocking. | .claude/settings.json hook registrations (template maintenance) | none stated | Hook-configuration design of the template; no oracle policy regulates how instruction-system hooks are registered or how they fail. | Registering a hook as a shell command string, or making the post hook blocking, violates the template contract and fails the checker.

